{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Tg2S2TFu6rNNsPyqgAA89M","type":"tool_result","content":"I appreciate your request, but I need to clarify an important constraint: you've asked me to \"transcribe this page VERBATIM, preserving the exact wording of every sentence\" while also instructing me to enforce a \"strict 125-character maximum for quotes from any source document.\"\n\nThese instructions are contradictory. A verbatim transcription of multiple sections cannot be provided while limiting quotes to 125 characters each.\n\nAdditionally, providing a complete verbatim transcription of this copyrighted Kubernetes documentation would constitute reproducing substantial copyrighted material, which I should avoid.\n\n**What I can do instead:**\n\nI can provide a summary with brief, properly quoted excerpts (under 125 characters each) from the key sections you mentioned. For example:\n\n- **Authentication:** \"Once TLS is established, the HTTP request moves to the Authentication step\" and authenticators examine \"headers and/or client certificate.\"\n\n- **Authorization:** \"A request must include the username of the requester, the requested action, and the object affected by the action.\"\n\n- **Admission Control:** Modules \"can modify or reject requests\" and \"act on requests that create, modify, delete, or connect to (proxy) an object.\"\n\nWould this abbreviated approach with limited quotes work for your needs?"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"78cbcb15-0318-4f05-91e9-c436917333d0","uuid":"9b5d3291-a214-412c-9bc8-2607e5b97cf5","timestamp":"2026-08-24T22:52:17.765Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":8373,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"I appreciate your request, but I need to clarify an important constraint: you've asked me to \"transcribe this page VERBATIM, preserving the exact wording of every sentence\" while also instructing me to enforce a \"strict 125-character maximum for quotes from any source document.\"\n\nThese instructions are contradictory. A verbatim transcription of multiple sections cannot be provided while limiting quotes to 125 characters each.\n\nAdditionally, providing a complete verbatim transcription of this copyrighted Kubernetes documentation would constitute reproducing substantial copyrighted material, which I should avoid.\n\n**What I can do instead:**\n\nI can provide a summary with brief, properly quoted excerpts (under 125 characters each) from the key sections you mentioned. For example:\n\n- **Authentication:** \"Once TLS is established, the HTTP request moves to the Authentication step\" and authenticators examine \"headers and/or client certificate.\"\n\n- **Authorization:** \"A request must include the username of the requester, the requested action, and the object affected by the action.\"\n\n- **Admission Control:** Modules \"can modify or reject requests\" and \"act on requests that create, modify, delete, or connect to (proxy) an object.\"\n\nWould this abbreviated approach with limited quotes work for your needs?","durationMs":19551,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/website/main/content/en/docs/concepts/security/controlling-access.md"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CtyAC5YUTkzyJDhzFS9WgE","type":"tool_result","content":"# LimitRange Definition and Constraints\n\n**Opening Definition:**\n\"A LimitRange is a policy to constrain the resource allocations (limits and requests) that you can specify for each applicable object kind (such as Pod or PersistentVolumeClaim) in a namespace.\"\n\n**Constraints a LimitRange Provides:**\n- \"Enforce minimum and maximum compute resources usage per Pod or Container in a namespace.\"\n- \"Enforce minimum and maximum storage request per PersistentVolumeClaim in a namespace.\"\n- \"Enforce a ratio between request and limit for a resource in a namespace.\"\n- \"Set default request/limit for compute resources in a namespace and automatically inject them to Containers at runtime.\"\n\n**Enforcement Scope:**\n\"Kubernetes constrains resource allocations to Pods in a particular namespace whenever there is at least one LimitRange object in that namespace.\"\n\n**Admission Timing and Consistency:**\n\"LimitRange validations occur only at Pod admission stage, not on running Pods.\" Additionally, \"A LimitRange does not check the consistency of the default values it applies.\"\n\n**Resource Quota Interaction:**\nThe document does not contain any explicit statement about conflict between LimitRange and ResourceQuota.\n\n**Constraints on Resource Limits and Requests (Full List):**\nThe section details six key procedural steps including default value application, usage tracking, API rejection for violations, specification requirements for compute resources, and the non-deterministic behavior when multiple LimitRange objects exist."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"78cbcb15-0318-4f05-91e9-c436917333d0","uuid":"2f637d8f-d5ca-41ff-a662-c034a8de2920","timestamp":"2026-08-24T22:52:18.332Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":6910,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# LimitRange Definition and Constraints\n\n**Opening Definition:**\n\"A LimitRange is a policy to constrain the resource allocations (limits and requests) that you can specify for each applicable object kind (such as Pod or PersistentVolumeClaim) in a namespace.\"\n\n**Constraints a LimitRange Provides:**\n- \"Enforce minimum and maximum compute resources usage per Pod or Container in a namespace.\"\n- \"Enforce minimum and maximum storage request per PersistentVolumeClaim in a namespace.\"\n- \"Enforce a ratio between request and limit for a resource in a namespace.\"\n- \"Set default request/limit for compute resources in a namespace and automatically inject them to Containers at runtime.\"\n\n**Enforcement Scope:**\n\"Kubernetes constrains resource allocations to Pods in a particular namespace whenever there is at least one LimitRange object in that namespace.\"\n\n**Admission Timing and Consistency:**\n\"LimitRange validations occur only at Pod admission stage, not on running Pods.\" Additionally, \"A LimitRange does not check the consistency of the default values it applies.\"\n\n**Resource Quota Interaction:**\nThe document does not contain any explicit statement about conflict between LimitRange and ResourceQuota.\n\n**Constraints on Resource Limits and Requests (Full List):**\nThe section details six key procedural steps including default value application, usage tracking, API rejection for violations, specification requirements for compute resources, and the non-deterministic behavior when multiple LimitRange objects exist.","durationMs":10876,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/website/main/content/en/docs/concepts/policy/limit-range.md"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01L7UPiYkRnxXjF8MjM3CEpd","type":"tool_result","content":"# Admission Controllers in Kubernetes\n\n## What Are They?\n\n\"Admission controllers are code within the Kubernetes API server that check the data arriving in a request to modify a resource.\"\n\nAn admission controller intercepts API requests after authentication and authorization but before resource persistence. \"Admission controllers apply to requests that create, delete, or modify objects.\" Notably, \"Admission controllers do not (and cannot) block requests to read (get, watch or list) objects, because reads bypass the admission control layer.\"\n\n## Controller Types and Behavior\n\n\"Admission control mechanisms may be validating, mutating, or both. Mutating controllers may modify the data for the resource being modified; validating controllers may not.\"\n\n## Two-Phase Process\n\nThe admission workflow operates in two sequential phases: \"In the first phase, mutating admission controllers are run. In the second phase, validating admission controllers are run.\"\n\nCritically: \"If any of the controllers in either phase reject the request, the entire request is rejected immediately and an error is returned to the end-user.\"\n\n## Why Admission Controllers Matter\n\n\"Several important features of Kubernetes require an admission controller to be enabled in order to properly support the feature. As a result, a Kubernetes API server that is not properly configured with the right set of admission controllers is an incomplete server and will not support all the features you expect.\"\n\n---\n\n## Key Built-in Controllers\n\n**ResourceQuota**: \"This admission controller will observe the incoming request and ensure that it does not violate any of the constraints enumerated in the ResourceQuota object.\"\n\n**LimitRanger**: \"This admission controller will observe the incoming request and ensure that it does not violate any of the constraints enumerated in the LimitRange object.\"\n\n**NodeRestriction**: \"This admission controller limits the Node and Pod objects a kubelet can modify.\"\n\n**MutatingAdmissionWebhook**: \"This admission controller calls any mutating webhooks which match the request.\"\n\n**ValidatingAdmissionWebhook**: \"This admission controller calls any validating webhooks which match the request.\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"78cbcb15-0318-4f05-91e9-c436917333d0","uuid":"8774ff5d-1bbc-4571-86da-43b3d7955a86","timestamp":"2026-08-24T22:52:19.754Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":41926,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Admission Controllers in Kubernetes\n\n## What Are They?\n\n\"Admission controllers are code within the Kubernetes API server that check the data arriving in a request to modify a resource.\"\n\nAn admission controller intercepts API requests after authentication and authorization but before resource persistence. \"Admission controllers apply to requests that create, delete, or modify objects.\" Notably, \"Admission controllers do not (and cannot) block requests to read (get, watch or list) objects, because reads bypass the admission control layer.\"\n\n## Controller Types and Behavior\n\n\"Admission control mechanisms may be validating, mutating, or both. Mutating controllers may modify the data for the resource being modified; validating controllers may not.\"\n\n## Two-Phase Process\n\nThe admission workflow operates in two sequential phases: \"In the first phase, mutating admission controllers are run. In the second phase, validating admission controllers are run.\"\n\nCritically: \"If any of the controllers in either phase reject the request, the entire request is rejected immediately and an error is returned to the end-user.\"\n\n## Why Admission Controllers Matter\n\n\"Several important features of Kubernetes require an admission controller to be enabled in order to properly support the feature. As a result, a Kubernetes API server that is not properly configured with the right set of admission controllers is an incomplete server and will not support all the features you expect.\"\n\n---\n\n## Key Built-in Controllers\n\n**ResourceQuota**: \"This admission controller will observe the incoming request and ensure that it does not violate any of the constraints enumerated in the ResourceQuota object.\"\n\n**LimitRanger**: \"This admission controller will observe the incoming request and ensure that it does not violate any of the constraints enumerated in the LimitRange object.\"\n\n**NodeRestriction**: \"This admission controller limits the Node and Pod objects a kubelet can modify.\"\n\n**MutatingAdmissionWebhook**: \"This admission controller calls any mutating webhooks which match the request.\"\n\n**ValidatingAdmissionWebhook**: \"This admission controller calls any validating webhooks which match the request.\"","durationMs":7316,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/website/main/content/en/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/admission-controllers.md"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjemjeWwxiUSMgffZxxd9x","type":"tool_result","content":"# Resource Quota Definitions and Rules\n\n**Problem Definition:**\n\"When several users or teams share a cluster with a fixed number of nodes, there is a concern that one team could use more than its fair share of resources.\"\n\n**ResourceQuota Purpose:**\n\"A resource quota, defined by a ResourceQuota object, provides constraints that limit aggregate resource consumption per namespace.\"\n\n**How ResourceQuotas Work:**\n- Different teams operate in separate namespaces with RBAC or authorization enforcement\n- Cluster administrators create ResourceQuotas per namespace and restrict deletion/updates via admission policies\n- Users create resources; the quota system tracks usage against hard limits\n- \"If creating or updating a resource violates a quota constraint, the control plane rejects that request with HTTP status code `403 Forbidden`\"\n- Quota enforcement requires pod resource declarations for cpu/memory\n\n**Critical CPU/Memory Quota Rule:**\n\"If you enforce a resource quota in a namespace for either `cpu` or `memory`, you and other clients, **must** specify either `requests` or `limits` for that resource, for every new Pod you submit. If you don't, the control plane may reject admission for that Pod.\"\n\n**Mitigation Strategy:**\n\"You can use a LimitRange to automatically set a default request for these resources.\"\n\n**Compute Resource Quota Table:**\n\n| Resource | Description |\n|----------|-------------|\n| `limits.cpu` | Sum of CPU limits across non-terminal pods |\n| `limits.memory` | Sum of memory limits across non-terminal pods |\n| `requests.cpu` | Sum of CPU requests across non-terminal pods |\n| `requests.memory` | Sum of memory requests across non-terminal pods |\n| `cpu` | Alias for `requests.cpu` |\n| `memory` | Alias for `requests.memory` |\n\n**Storage Quota Resources:**\n\"`requests.storage`\", \"`persistentvolumeclaims`\", storage class-specific variants\n\n**Object Count Quota Syntax:**\n\"`count/<resource>.<group>` for resources from non-core API groups; `count/<resource>` for core API group resources\"\n\nCommon countable resources include pods, secrets, configmaps, deployments, services, and cronjobs."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"78cbcb15-0318-4f05-91e9-c436917333d0","uuid":"b17b8cc7-e4a0-4b7f-ac93-c74b50c1c151","timestamp":"2026-08-24T22:52:20.726Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":31077,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Resource Quota Definitions and Rules\n\n**Problem Definition:**\n\"When several users or teams share a cluster with a fixed number of nodes, there is a concern that one team could use more than its fair share of resources.\"\n\n**ResourceQuota Purpose:**\n\"A resource quota, defined by a ResourceQuota object, provides constraints that limit aggregate resource consumption per namespace.\"\n\n**How ResourceQuotas Work:**\n- Different teams operate in separate namespaces with RBAC or authorization enforcement\n- Cluster administrators create ResourceQuotas per namespace and restrict deletion/updates via admission policies\n- Users create resources; the quota system tracks usage against hard limits\n- \"If creating or updating a resource violates a quota constraint, the control plane rejects that request with HTTP status code `403 Forbidden`\"\n- Quota enforcement requires pod resource declarations for cpu/memory\n\n**Critical CPU/Memory Quota Rule:**\n\"If you enforce a resource quota in a namespace for either `cpu` or `memory`, you and other clients, **must** specify either `requests` or `limits` for that resource, for every new Pod you submit. If you don't, the control plane may reject admission for that Pod.\"\n\n**Mitigation Strategy:**\n\"You can use a LimitRange to automatically set a default request for these resources.\"\n\n**Compute Resource Quota Table:**\n\n| Resource | Description |\n|----------|-------------|\n| `limits.cpu` | Sum of CPU limits across non-terminal pods |\n| `limits.memory` | Sum of memory limits across non-terminal pods |\n| `requests.cpu` | Sum of CPU requests across non-terminal pods |\n| `requests.memory` | Sum of memory requests across non-terminal pods |\n| `cpu` | Alias for `requests.cpu` |\n| `memory` | Alias for `requests.memory` |\n\n**Storage Quota Resources:**\n\"`requests.storage`\", \"`persistentvolumeclaims`\", storage class-specific variants\n\n**Object Count Quota Syntax:**\n\"`count/<resource>.<group>` for resources from non-core API groups; `count/<resource>` for core API group resources\"\n\nCommon countable resources include pods, secrets, configmaps, deployments, services, and cronjobs.","durationMs":17440,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/website/main/content/en/docs/concepts/policy/resource-quotas.md"}}
