{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UBGPZFd7ncwHJocMbJSZhK","type":"tool_result","content":"# Kubernetes Auditing: Key Definitions and Concepts\n\n**(1) Opening Definition** — From \"Overview\" section:\n\"Kubernetes _auditing_ provides a security-relevant, chronological set of records documenting the sequence of actions in a cluster.\"\n\n**(2) Questions Auditing Answers** — From \"Overview\" section:\n\"Auditing allows cluster administrators to answer the following questions: what happened? when did it happen? who initiated it? on what did it happen? where was it observed? from where was it initiated? to where was it going?\"\n\n**(3) Audit Lifecycle** — From \"Audit policy\" section:\n\"Audit records begin their lifecycle inside the kube-apiserver component. Each request on each stage of its execution generates an audit event.\"\n\n**(4) Audit Stages** — From \"Audit policy\" section:\n- `RequestReceived` - \"The stage for events generated as soon as the audit handler receives the request\"\n- `ResponseStarted` - \"Once the response headers are sent, but before the response body is sent\"\n- `ResponseComplete` - \"The response body has been completed and no more bytes will be sent\"\n- `Panic` - \"Events generated when a panic occurred\"\n\n**(5) Audit Policy & Levels** — From \"Audit policy\" section:\n\"Audit policy defines rules about what events should be recorded and what data they should include.\" Levels include `None`, `Metadata`, `Request`, and `RequestResponse` with their respective logging scope definitions.\n\n**(6) Audit Backends** — From \"Audit backends\" section:\n\"Out of the box, the kube-apiserver provides two backends: Log backend, which writes events into the filesystem [and] Webhook backend, which sends events to an external HTTP API.\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"78cbcb15-0318-4f05-91e9-c436917333d0","uuid":"70406844-366b-41e2-841f-46cfca9f9872","timestamp":"2026-08-24T22:52:59.639Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":12710,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Kubernetes Auditing: Key Definitions and Concepts\n\n**(1) Opening Definition** — From \"Overview\" section:\n\"Kubernetes _auditing_ provides a security-relevant, chronological set of records documenting the sequence of actions in a cluster.\"\n\n**(2) Questions Auditing Answers** — From \"Overview\" section:\n\"Auditing allows cluster administrators to answer the following questions: what happened? when did it happen? who initiated it? on what did it happen? where was it observed? from where was it initiated? to where was it going?\"\n\n**(3) Audit Lifecycle** — From \"Audit policy\" section:\n\"Audit records begin their lifecycle inside the kube-apiserver component. Each request on each stage of its execution generates an audit event.\"\n\n**(4) Audit Stages** — From \"Audit policy\" section:\n- `RequestReceived` - \"The stage for events generated as soon as the audit handler receives the request\"\n- `ResponseStarted` - \"Once the response headers are sent, but before the response body is sent\"\n- `ResponseComplete` - \"The response body has been completed and no more bytes will be sent\"\n- `Panic` - \"Events generated when a panic occurred\"\n\n**(5) Audit Policy & Levels** — From \"Audit policy\" section:\n\"Audit policy defines rules about what events should be recorded and what data they should include.\" Levels include `None`, `Metadata`, `Request`, and `RequestResponse` with their respective logging scope definitions.\n\n**(6) Audit Backends** — From \"Audit backends\" section:\n\"Out of the box, the kube-apiserver provides two backends: Log backend, which writes events into the filesystem [and] Webhook backend, which sends events to an external HTTP API.\"","durationMs":5030,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/website/main/content/en/docs/tasks/debug/debug-cluster/audit.md"}}
