{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Tdihs9Y91rNAd6YPKEaUoc","type":"tool_result","content":"# Reserve Compute Resources for System Daemons: Extract\n\n**(1) Opening paragraph on node capacity issue:**\n\n\"Kubernetes nodes can be scheduled to `Capacity`. Pods can consume all the available capacity on a node by default. This is an issue because nodes typically run quite a few system daemons that power the OS and Kubernetes itself.\" (Overview section)\n\n**(2) Definition of Node Allocatable:**\n\n\"'Allocatable' on a Kubernetes node is defined as the amount of compute resources that are available for pods.\" (Node Allocatable section)\n\n**(3) Definition of kube-reserved:**\n\n\"`kubeReserved` is meant to capture resource reservation for kubernetes system daemons like the `kubelet`, `container runtime`, etc. It is not meant to reserve resources for system daemons that are run as pods.\" (Kube Reserved section)\n\n**(4) Definition of system-reserved:**\n\n\"`systemReserved` is meant to capture resource reservation for OS system daemons like `sshd`, `udev`, etc.\" and should \"reserve `memory` for the `kernel`\" and \"reserving resources for user login sessions is also recommended.\" (System Reserved section)\n\n**(5) Eviction thresholds and memory:**\n\n\"By reserving some memory via `evictionHard` setting, the `kubelet` attempts to evict pods whenever memory availability on the node drops below the reserved value.\" (Eviction Thresholds section)\n\n**(6) Relationship between these components:**\n\n**No textual equation provided.** The document includes a diagram labeled \"node capacity\" but provides no TEXT statement describing how Capacity, kube-reserved, system-reserved, eviction-threshold, and Allocatable mathematically relate."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"78cbcb15-0318-4f05-91e9-c436917333d0","uuid":"c449426d-53a2-49d8-9a58-3221372587e7","timestamp":"2026-08-24T22:53:05.924Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":12198,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Reserve Compute Resources for System Daemons: Extract\n\n**(1) Opening paragraph on node capacity issue:**\n\n\"Kubernetes nodes can be scheduled to `Capacity`. Pods can consume all the available capacity on a node by default. This is an issue because nodes typically run quite a few system daemons that power the OS and Kubernetes itself.\" (Overview section)\n\n**(2) Definition of Node Allocatable:**\n\n\"'Allocatable' on a Kubernetes node is defined as the amount of compute resources that are available for pods.\" (Node Allocatable section)\n\n**(3) Definition of kube-reserved:**\n\n\"`kubeReserved` is meant to capture resource reservation for kubernetes system daemons like the `kubelet`, `container runtime`, etc. It is not meant to reserve resources for system daemons that are run as pods.\" (Kube Reserved section)\n\n**(4) Definition of system-reserved:**\n\n\"`systemReserved` is meant to capture resource reservation for OS system daemons like `sshd`, `udev`, etc.\" and should \"reserve `memory` for the `kernel`\" and \"reserving resources for user login sessions is also recommended.\" (System Reserved section)\n\n**(5) Eviction thresholds and memory:**\n\n\"By reserving some memory via `evictionHard` setting, the `kubelet` attempts to evict pods whenever memory availability on the node drops below the reserved value.\" (Eviction Thresholds section)\n\n**(6) Relationship between these components:**\n\n**No textual equation provided.** The document includes a diagram labeled \"node capacity\" but provides no TEXT statement describing how Capacity, kube-reserved, system-reserved, eviction-threshold, and Allocatable mathematically relate.","durationMs":4764,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/website/main/content/en/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/reserve-compute-resources.md"}}
