{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XpG24MAAk2Angb3yYpyf2K","type":"tool_result","content":"# kubectl drain Overview\n\n**(1) Opening description** — From \"Use `kubectl drain` to remove a node from service\":\n\"You can use `kubectl drain` to safely evict all of your pods from a node before you perform maintenance on the node (e.g. kernel upgrade, hardware maintenance, etc.). Safe evictions allow the pod's containers to gracefully terminate and will respect the PodDisruptionBudgets you have specified.\"\n\n**(2) Cordon statement** — Not explicitly present in the document. While the content mentions marking nodes \"unschedulable\" and later discusses `kubectl uncordon`, there is no direct sentence stating that `kubectl drain` cordons the node as part of its operation.\n\n**(3) DaemonSet pods during drain** — From \"Use `kubectl drain` to remove a node from service\":\n\"If there are pods managed by a DaemonSet, you will need to specify `--ignore-daemonsets` with `kubectl` to successfully drain the node.\"\n\n**(4) Powering down and returning to service** — From \"Use `kubectl drain` to remove a node from service\":\n\"Once it returns (without giving an error), you can power down the node (or equivalently, if on a cloud platform, delete the virtual machine backing the node).\" And: \"you need to run `kubectl uncordon <node name>` afterwards to tell Kubernetes that it can resume scheduling new pods onto the node.\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"78cbcb15-0318-4f05-91e9-c436917333d0","uuid":"63528561-cc13-4502-b2fc-f78d72432b41","timestamp":"2026-08-24T22:53:48.329Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":5925,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# kubectl drain Overview\n\n**(1) Opening description** — From \"Use `kubectl drain` to remove a node from service\":\n\"You can use `kubectl drain` to safely evict all of your pods from a node before you perform maintenance on the node (e.g. kernel upgrade, hardware maintenance, etc.). Safe evictions allow the pod's containers to gracefully terminate and will respect the PodDisruptionBudgets you have specified.\"\n\n**(2) Cordon statement** — Not explicitly present in the document. While the content mentions marking nodes \"unschedulable\" and later discusses `kubectl uncordon`, there is no direct sentence stating that `kubectl drain` cordons the node as part of its operation.\n\n**(3) DaemonSet pods during drain** — From \"Use `kubectl drain` to remove a node from service\":\n\"If there are pods managed by a DaemonSet, you will need to specify `--ignore-daemonsets` with `kubectl` to successfully drain the node.\"\n\n**(4) Powering down and returning to service** — From \"Use `kubectl drain` to remove a node from service\":\n\"Once it returns (without giving an error), you can power down the node (or equivalently, if on a cloud platform, delete the virtual machine backing the node).\" And: \"you need to run `kubectl uncordon <node name>` afterwards to tell Kubernetes that it can resume scheduling new pods onto the node.\"","durationMs":3628,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/website/main/content/en/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/safely-drain-node.md"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UUz2JT5vqxqSNsoJVaKaTw","type":"tool_result","content":"# API-Initiated Eviction: Key Statements\n\n**(1) Definition (Introduction section):**\n\"You can request eviction by calling the Eviction API directly, or programmatically using a client of the API server, like the `kubectl drain` command. This creates an `Eviction` object, which causes the API server to terminate the Pod.\"\n\n**(2) Methods of requesting eviction (Introduction section):**\nSame as above—covers both direct API calls and programmatic approaches via client tools.\n\n**(3) API server behavior upon eviction request (Introduction section):**\n\"Using the API to create an Eviction object for a Pod is like performing a policy-controlled `DELETE` operation on the Pod.\"\n\nAdditional detail from \"How API-initiated eviction works\": \"the API server performs admission checks and responds in one of the following ways\" with outcomes including `200 OK` allowing deletion or `429`/`500` responses denying it.\n\n**(4) Respect for PodDisruptionBudgets and terminationGracePeriodSeconds (Introduction section):**\n\"API-initiated evictions respect your configured `PodDisruptionBudgets` and `terminationGracePeriodSeconds`.\"\n\n**(5) Contrast with node-pressure eviction:**\nNo contrast is explicitly drawn in this document. Node-pressure eviction is mentioned only in the \"whatsnext\" section as a related topic to learn about separately."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"78cbcb15-0318-4f05-91e9-c436917333d0","uuid":"47eb2494-e2b5-447f-9c7d-b11635cfc06b","timestamp":"2026-08-24T22:53:51.744Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":5035,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# API-Initiated Eviction: Key Statements\n\n**(1) Definition (Introduction section):**\n\"You can request eviction by calling the Eviction API directly, or programmatically using a client of the API server, like the `kubectl drain` command. This creates an `Eviction` object, which causes the API server to terminate the Pod.\"\n\n**(2) Methods of requesting eviction (Introduction section):**\nSame as above—covers both direct API calls and programmatic approaches via client tools.\n\n**(3) API server behavior upon eviction request (Introduction section):**\n\"Using the API to create an Eviction object for a Pod is like performing a policy-controlled `DELETE` operation on the Pod.\"\n\nAdditional detail from \"How API-initiated eviction works\": \"the API server performs admission checks and responds in one of the following ways\" with outcomes including `200 OK` allowing deletion or `429`/`500` responses denying it.\n\n**(4) Respect for PodDisruptionBudgets and terminationGracePeriodSeconds (Introduction section):**\n\"API-initiated evictions respect your configured `PodDisruptionBudgets` and `terminationGracePeriodSeconds`.\"\n\n**(5) Contrast with node-pressure eviction:**\nNo contrast is explicitly drawn in this document. Node-pressure eviction is mentioned only in the \"whatsnext\" section as a related topic to learn about separately.","durationMs":4255,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/website/main/content/en/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/api-eviction.md"}}
